Modeling a human-like decision-making strategy in ADS necessitates a deep understanding of fundamental ADS perception functions. Considering that traffic scenarios deemed safe by AVs might be perceived as unsafe by human drivers [12], recent studies have focused on the disparity in hazard perception between ADS and humans. Xu et al. (2022) utilized a human-in-the-loop study to analyze driving pattern differences, revealing significant gaps in risk perception between AI and human drivers. Notably, the ADS exhibited a higher tolerance for risk, contributing to its ability to maintain shorter headways [13]. Following this clue, Wang et al. (2020) conducted an empirical study in Xian, China, translating human drivers’ risk perceptions during lane changes into a human-like lane change model for ADS. Their results indicated that incorporating such a model could enhance user acceptance of AV technology in lane-change scenarios [14]. Furthermore, He et al. (2022) established a link between human-perceived risks and trust in AV technology, focusing their simulation study on motorway merging and hard braking events. Their findings underscored the positive impact of a human-centered automation design in boosting user adoption of AV technology [15].
On the other hand, the heterogeneity of human perception and behavior poses a challenge to the pursuit of a humancentered collaboration model in advanced intelligent systems. Xing et al. (2021) suggested that such an approach might inadvertently impair the development of situational awareness capabilities in these systems [16]. Consequently, the role of the Human-Machine Interface (HMI) in fostering mutual understanding between humans and vehicular autonomy has become a key area of research [17]. While vehicle autonomy is expected to adaptively make decisions that are either humanlike or accepted by humans, frequent interaction with HMI may not necessarily transition the role of individuals from “driver” to “passenger” in autonomous driving. As Carsten et al. (2019) highlighted, this interaction could paradoxically increase driver workload and reduce situational awareness

