II. GENERATION OF TESTING SCENARIOS FOR AUTONOMOUS DRIVING
A. Development of the Naturalistic Driving Environment
At the core of the driving environment is a co-simulation strategy that integrates virtual environments with traffic microsimulation. Specifically, we utilize a range of scenes provided by CARLA [19], comprising 3 large-scale maps (over 100 km2), 3 moderate-scale maps (around 50 km2), and 5 small-scale maps (around 20 km2). These maps form the basis of the virtual environment. Corresponding real-world traffic flows, rules, and management strategies are modeled in VISSIM. In addition to the Wiedemann 99 behavioral model [23], the Reciprocal Velocity Obstacles (RVO) algorithm [24] and a multi-layer behavior model strategy are incorporated into CARLA. This integration aims to enhance the realism of simulated road users, including the modeling of road users’ misconduct. Moreover, the integration of meteorological data within CARLA facilitates the simulation of dynamic and realistic weather conditions. In conjunction with traffic dynamics, this feature enables the recreation of a broad spectrum of driving scenarios. As a result, a naturalistic simulation

