III. EXPERIMENT DESIGN
This study employs a comparative experimental design to assess and integrate human riders’ perceptions into the safety performance of the fine-tuned ADS, to improve overall safety performance. To this end, the experiments are conducted in two parts, involving two groups with subjects evenly distributed across both. Notably, the behavioral results identified in group one are quantified and applied to the tests for group two. Apart from this, the testing scenarios for both groups are essentially identical. In general, the controlled variables in this study are the testing scenarios, attributes of the sample population, and the duration of the experimental period. Details are provided in the following subsections.
A. Sample Determination
In this study, we develop over 970 autonomous driving scenarios across 11 CARLA maps for human-in-the-loop analysis. These scenarios are classified based on road type, traffic flow, weather, and lighting conditions. Each scenario involves a driving duration of 120 to 150 seconds under varying conditions. To comprehensively represent these scenarios’ characteristics, the ideal sample size was calculated to be 276 for a 95% confidence level. However, due to constraints, we opted for an alternative sampling method and recruited a total of 46 participants (26 males and 20 females), each experiencing 6 different scenarios during the experiment.
The participant pool recruited for this study reflects a diverse range of attributes, including varying genders, a wide age spectrum (M ean = 29.43, SD =6.44), and different levels of driving and VR experience. Participants are assigned to two different experimental groups randomly, with their attributes evenly distributed. Particularly, each group is composed as follows: 4 non-drivers aged between 18 and 22; 6 less experienced drivers with 1 to 3 years of driving experience, aged between 20 and 25; 7 general drivers with 5 to 10 years of driving experience, aged between 24 and 35; and 6 experienced drivers with 10 to 20 years of driving experience, aged between 28 and 49. Furthermore, all participants have prior VR experience, ranging from a minimum of three uses to more than ten.

B. Experiment Setup
The experiment utilizes a portable VR driving simulator (Fig. 3), comprising the Logitech G923 driving set and HTC VIVE Pro Eye. The simulator runs on a PC equipped with a high-performance NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 graphics card, ensuring smooth and efficient operation of both the ADS training and the VR experiment. Within the defined experimental framework, the experiments for group one and group two are conducted separately. The primary distinction in the experiment settings between the two groups lies in the Formal VR Experiment phase. After evaluating the performance of participants in Group One, tailored visual and audio assistance systems (Fig. 3) are developed and integrated into the scenarios for Group Two. In this study, interventions that undermine transport efficiency, expand conflict zones, or potentially lead to road accidents are categorized as unnecessary, compared to the performance of the fine-tuned ADS. A key factor driving these interventions is the discrepancy in safety perceptions between the ADS and human riders, posing a significant obstacle to widespread adoption of AV technologies. Our research contributes a novel approach to mitigating this perception gap by using visual and auditory cues to discourage unnecessary interventions. As depicted in Fig. 3, when the ADS approaches a situation that could induce an intervention, visual effects are displayed, featuring the messages “Safe Condition” in black and “No Intervention Needed” in red. Simultaneously, an audio message stating “Safe Condition, No Intervention Needed” is played twice at a slow pace, using a female voice. This integration aims to serve as a preliminary approach to reduce unnecessary rider interventions during autonomous driving, thereby enhancing overall safety. Consequently, there is a one-month gap between the experiments conducted with group one and group two to allow for these adjustments.
However, both groups follow the same procedure:
1) Pre-experiment Survey (3-5 min): Before the experiment, each participant must complete a Simulation Sickness Questionnaire (SSQ) [27]. This is to confirm their suitability for participating in the VR experiment. Additionally, the survey results serve as a baseline for assessing any side effects of the experiment in terms of simulation sickness levels.
2) Formal VR Experiment (15-20 min): Each participant is assigned six driving scenarios, randomly selected from the 11 available maps. It is ensured that no participant encounters repetitive scenarios. Each scenario, lasting 120 to 150 seconds, involves Level 4 autonomous driving under various conditions. During the experiments, participants have two options for initiating intervention behavior (Fig. 4). They can deactivate autopilot mode by pressing a designated button on the steering wheel, or they can directly intervene by turning the steering wheel or stepping on the brake pedal whenever they feel unsafe or uncomfortable. To reactivate autopilot mode, participants can either press the autopilot button again or, If there is no intervention for three consecutive seconds, the ADS resumes autopilot mode. Participants have the

