C. Data Collection and Processing
During the experiment, both objective and subjective data are collected. Subjective data is gathered through questionnaire surveys, which assess participants’ simulation sickness levels and their feedback on the simulation study. Objective data collection encompasses several components: (1) observed intentions of intervention, (2) recorded execution of intervention, and (3) participants’ eye movement and gaze patterns. The recorded execution of intervention is automatically documented by CARLA during the experiment, and participants’ eye movement and gaze patterns are captured using the VR goggle integrated with Tobii eye-tracking SDKs [30]. In terms of the intention of intervention, it is classified into three categories: a. Slight intention, indicated by either turning or braking for less than 10 seconds. b. Moderate intention,

indicated by both turning and braking for over 10 seconds. c. Strong intention, indicated by both turning and braking for most of the experiment.
In this study, the collected objective data provides the direct and most relevant information with respect to the user adoption during the autonomous driving, in which the measured perception plays a critical role. Theoretically, actions can be induced according to the triggers perceived, and the link between visual attentions and subsequent actions can be modelled and quantified via modelling.
For the specific needs of this experiment, we utilized the visual attention model to analyze the eye patterns of group one participants [31]. This model quantifies three primary features — color, intensity, and orientation — during the human perception process, measuring the correlation between eye movements and cognitive processing in free-viewing tasks. Essentially, the model processes the human view as an image, which is then subsampled into a Gaussian pyramid characterized by features such as Red (R), Green (G), Blue (B), Yellow (Y ), Intensity (I), and local orientations (O). This allows the identification of “attention attractors” based on specific “feature maps” of views. These maps are linearly combined and normalized to form “conspicuity maps”, which guide the focus of attention. Mathematically, these “conspicuity maps” are derived as a sum of the “feature maps”:

