IV. RESULTS AND ANALYSIS
This section presents an analysis of the experiment results. The comparative data on environmental awareness between participants and the ADS provides valuable insights into the acceptance of AV technology. Additionally, it sheds light on how individuals behaviorally adapt during autonomous driving.
A. Inconsistency in Safety Perception between ADS and Human Riders
Fig. 5 illustrates the perceived differences in environmental awareness between human drivers, non-drivers, and the ADS. It reveals that human drivers, particularly experienced ones, exhibit greater caution than the ADS. This heightened sensitivity is especially noticeable in responses to the braking actions of the preceding vehicle, traffic signals, and the sudden entry of other vehicles. As depicted in Fig. 5(a), participant eye movements rank the features drawing their attention during the experiment. Unlike the ADS, which maintains a stable focus on specific features affecting operation safety, human drivers often scan a wider range of potential hazards, even though traveling at higher speeds (Fig. 5(b)). This divergent perception, as contrasted with the reinforcement learning result (i.e., the ADS) that prioritizes the most relevant obstacles, is evident in Fig. 5(c). The ADS employs a processing system that extracts key characteristics of its surroundings, such as pedestrian skeletons, to identify and avoid potential obstacles. This object-oriented approach to perceiving environmental features is fundamentally different from human perception, even though it is designed to ensure safety.
As a result, when sitting in a high-level ADS, human drivers anticipate a slower speed when there are top-ranked attention-affecting features in a scenario, preferring a smoother reduction rate over the ADS’s more efficient linear control algorithm. As shown in Fig. 5(d), over a specified 10-second interval, human drivers tend to prefer initiating braking earlier. This would allow for a smoother reduction in speed compared to the more instantaneous braking performance of the ADS. Interestingly, the gaze patterns of non-drivers revealed a distinct tendency, as illustrated in the lower two graphics of Fig. 5(b). Unlike drivers, non-drivers tend to focus on the surrounding environment rather than on the driving task itself. Their gaze patterns resembled those of sightseers more than drivers, indicating a preference for the role of a passenger rather than that of a driver. This suggests that non-drivers are more inclined to enjoy the journey and the scenery, rather than engage in judgment and decision-making related to autonomous driving.

