C. Simulation Factors
Simulation sickness is recognized as a significant factor that negatively affects participant performance in simulator studies [33]–[35]. The SSQ serves as a comprehensive tool for monitoring the level of sickness in participants following exposure to VR experiments, finding extensive application across various simulation-based studies [33], [36], [37]. The SSQ categorizes symptoms into three main groups: nausea, which includes issues like vomiting and dizziness; oculomotor problems, such as eyestrain, blurred vision, and headaches; and disorientation, characterized by vertigo, imbalance, and a general sense of discomfort [27]. Its scoring system utilizes a principal factor analysis technique to accurately assess the overall intensity of simulation sickness and provides detailed

