V. CONCLUSIONS
This study underscores that individuals, particularly experienced drivers, are prone to intervene in high-level autonomous driving scenarios if they perceive a safety risk, despite the ADS maintaining an extremely low accident rate. In response, we propose a strategy aimed at preventing unnecessary interventions that could disrupt the ADS’s optimal functioning. This strategy, based on understanding human perception, employs visual and auditory cues to reassure riders about the nonnecessity of intervention. Our findings highlight the critical need for a human-centric approach in the development of ADS technology, such as during the Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) process. This is particularly vital in complex driving scenarios, including approaching traffic lights, responding to other vehicles cutting in, and maneuvering through dense traffic flows. A key insight is that maintaining a relatively low relative speed can significantly enhance rider comfort.
While advancements in multisensory systems have significantly improved the detection accuracy of ADS [38], the aspect of rider trust remains a critical challenge in the widespread adoption of AVs, as highlighted in recent studies [37], [39]. This aspect often receives less attention in the pursuit of achieving extreme precision in ADS functionality. Given that drivers are likely to be the primary and largest user group for AVs, it is crucial to reshape their perception and trust in ADS to encourage broader acceptance of AV technology. Our work reveals the limitations in the environmental awareness of the ADS as a result of the existing DRL strategy, contributing insights on enhancing user adoption of AVs, either by anticipating and preventing unnecessary interventions or by subtly shifting their focus from driving, thereby redefining their role from drivers to passengers.
We recognize the objective limitations of the present study concerning the sample size and composition. The selection criteria for participants may introduce bias, potentially limiting the generalizability of our findings on enhancing the ADS algorithm in terms of environmental awareness. Despite these constraints, our study explores a wide array of autonomous driving scenarios. This comprehensive approach has enabled us to identify key differences in perception between humans and the ADS, offering valuable insights for future research aimed at improving the ADS perception system.

